          "distinct": true,
          "id": "4d241979b920d50e8f80a103646c3d85df5cbc7f",
          "message": "feat(proof-methods): hand-written ACIR prototype for the integer literal opening (#6803)\n\n* proof-methods: hand-written ACIR prototype for the integer literal opening\n\nAdds proof-methods/acir: a generator that writes the ACIR for opening a\ncommitted xsd:integer literal with one variable-length BLAKE3 (two\ncompressions from XOR and RANGE black boxes, digit-count selectors) in place\nof the 20 fixed-length BLAKE3 black boxes that full_u64_check constrains.\nThe opcodes do not depend on the value. Includes the same algorithm written\nin Noir and the full_u64_check baseline, a run script that proves and\nverifies all three, a single-witness tamper check, and unit tests wired into\nthe proof-methods workflow.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_015CB7XiW3NDsggNCuDcuJJo\n\n* proof-methods/acir: bound XOR-with-zero operands to 32 bits\n\nThe x ^ 0 shortcut returned x without the 32-bit bound the XOR black box\nwould have imposed, leaving the four additions that meet the zero counter\nwords unbounded: a second carry then admitted a wrong hash. Emit RANGE(32)\nin the shortcut, test that every addition result is bounded by an XOR or a\n32-bit RANGE, and use run.sh bounds that do not reveal the values.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_015CB7XiW3NDsggNCuDcuJJo\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-11T00:37:33Z",
          "tree_id": "6f0a1bad7a5a545afb25d97059be097c08feb362",
          "url": "https://github.com/sparq-org/sparq/commit/4d241979b920d50e8f80a103646c3d85df5cbc7f"
